<ContentPage title="Trading House" subtitle="Economic self-sufficiency through smart commerce.">
  <p>
    The Trading House is BARK's economic engine — connecting local production with regional and global markets.
    It handles procurement, sales, logistics, and financial operations for the community. Below is real data
    on what we can trade, where, and at what margins.
  </p>

  <h2>Export Goods</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Specialty Coffee</strong> — Yungas coffee is Bolivia's primary specialty export. Current FOB (farm gate) price for specialty-grade Bolivian coffee: approximately <strong>$4.60/lb</strong>. Roasted and branded for direct-to-consumer sales, retail prices reach $25&ndash;45/lb. Bolivia's coffee production is small-scale and high-altitude, which commands premium prices in specialty markets.<sup>[1]</sup></li>
    <li><strong>Cacao</strong> — Bolivia exported approximately <strong>621 metric tons</strong> of cacao in 2023, valued at roughly <strong>$2.9 million</strong>. Bolivian cacao (especially wild Beniano and Alto Beni varieties) is increasingly recognized for quality. Bean-to-bar chocolate commands $8&ndash;15 per 70g bar at retail.<sup>[2]</sup></li>
    <li><strong>Tropical Fruits</strong> — Bolivia's fruit exports reached approximately <strong>$221 million</strong> nationally in recent years, with <strong>38% growth</strong> year-over-year. The Yungas produces passion fruit, papaya, avocado, banana, citrus, and cherimoya. Most is consumed domestically, meaning export channels are underdeveloped — an opportunity.<sup>[3]</sup></li>
    <li><strong>Essential Oils</strong> — The Yungas biodiversity supports distillation of eucalyptus, rosemary, lemongrass, and native aromatic species. Small-batch essential oils from identifiable origins command significant premiums in international markets.</li>
    <li><strong>Honey</strong> — Yungas honey from native stingless bees (Meliponini) and Apis mellifera is a niche product with growing demand. Organic, single-origin honey sells at premium prices.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Import Needs</h2>
  <p>
    BARK will need to import goods that cannot be produced locally, especially during the build-out phase.
    Strategic bulk procurement reduces per-unit costs significantly.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Solar equipment</strong> — Panels, inverters, batteries, charge controllers. Bolivia's government has committed <strong>$325 million</strong> to solar infrastructure with an additional <strong>$125 million</strong> from the World Bank, which may create domestic supply chains over time.<sup>[4]</sup></li>
    <li><strong>Construction materials</strong> — Cement, steel, glass, specialized fasteners. Available in La Paz but transport costs to Coroico add 15&ndash;25% to base prices.</li>
    <li><strong>Medical supplies</strong> — Basic pharmaceuticals, surgical supplies, diagnostic equipment. La Paz has medical supply markets, but specialized items may need international sourcing.</li>
    <li><strong>Processing machinery</strong> — Coffee roasters, cacao processing equipment, essential oil stills. Mostly imported from Brazil, Colombia, or Asia. Details in the <a href="/en/pillars/technopark">Techno Park</a> page.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Regional Trade Routes</h2>
  <p>
    BARK sits on the La Paz&ndash;Yungas corridor with access to Bolivia's main economic hubs:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>La Paz</strong> — 3&ndash;3.5 hours by road. Primary market for all goods. <strong>Mercado Rodriguez</strong> (5 km from city center) is the main wholesale market for Yungas agricultural products. International airport for exports.</li>
    <li><strong>Cochabamba</strong> — 8&ndash;10 hours by road (via La Paz or Oruro). Bolivia's agricultural processing center and the country's second-largest consumer market.</li>
    <li><strong>Santa Cruz</strong> — 12&ndash;14 hours by road. Bolivia's commercial capital and primary <strong>export gateway</strong> with the largest airport and connections to Brazilian logistics networks.</li>
    <li><strong>El Alto</strong> — Adjacent to La Paz. Bolivia's fastest-growing city and a major hub for informal trade, manufacturing, and the country's largest open-air market (Feria 16 de Julio).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Value-Added Processing</h2>
  <p>
    Raw materials leave as finished products wherever possible. Coffee is roasted and branded. Cacao becomes chocolate.
    Fruit becomes preserves and dried products. Essential oils are distilled and bottled. Each processing step
    adds 3&ndash;10x value and builds capability within the community. The <a href="/en/pillars/technopark">Techno Park</a> provides
    the machinery; the Trading House provides the market access.
  </p>

  <h2>Economic Integration</h2>
  <p>
    BARK doesn't compete with the local economy — it amplifies it. The Trading House creates channels that
    local producers can also use. Shared logistics, shared market access, shared learning.
    A rising tide approach. Local coffee farmers accessing specialty export markets through BARK's channels
    benefits everyone.
  </p>

  <h2>Financial Resilience</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Multi-currency operations</strong> — Boliviano (BOB) for local trade, USD for international commerce, crypto capabilities for cross-border payments avoiding banking friction</li>
    <li><strong>Barter networks</strong> — For crisis scenarios and local exchange where cash is unnecessary</li>
    <li><strong>Cooperative treasury</strong> — Managed under Bolivian cooperative law (Ley 356) with transparent accounting and member oversight</li>
  </ul>

